@martincostello Thanks for reporting this issue!

RDG enables the nullability context for generated files by adding `#nullable enable` in the file preamble. From my understanding, all source generated code needs to enable this since generated files don't implicitly carry the nullability context of the project.

```
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// <auto-generated>
//     This code was generated by a tool.
//
//     Changes to this file may cause incorrect behavior and will be lost if
//     the code is regenerated.
// </auto-generated>
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#nullable enable
```

There's definitely something going on here since I'm able to repro the issue but I think the generator is emitting the right code here.

@captainsafia Ignore that last comment. 

It's this line that's causing the issue:

```csharp
           var jsonBodyOrServiceTypeTuples = new (bool, Type)[] {
               #nullable disable
               (false, typeof(global::Microsoft.AspNetCore.Antiforgery.IAntiforgery)),
               #nullable restore
           };
```

The `#nullable restore` is resetting the context back to 'off', so everything below it is in a non-enabled context. Given that the generators owns the code below the line you should just change it to a `#nullable enable` to get the correct context back.
